Delta lake
==========
open source technology
storage framework/layer
enabling buiding lakehouse

tx or delta log

ordered records of every transaction performed on the table
single source of truth
Json file contain commit info:
	operation performed + predicates used (like any filters)
	data files affected (added + removed)
	
delta lake pros:
	brings ACID transactions to object storage
	handles scalable metadata
	full audit trail of all changes
	builds upon standard data formats: parquet + json

https://github.com/derar-alhussein/Databricks-Certified-Data-Engineer-Associate

create table employess
(id int, name string, salary DOUBLE);

insert into employess
values
(1,"adam",3500.0),
(2,"smith",4500.0),
(3,"arish",5500.0),
(4,"naik",6500.0)
(5,"mary",7500.0)
(6,"mike",8500.0)
select *from employess

describe detail employess //table metadata
	location : dbfs:/user/hive/warehouse/employess
	numfiles
	
%fs ls dbfs:/user/hive/warehouse/employess
it will create 4 files as our cluster has 4 cores

update employess
set salary = salary + 100
where name LIKE "a%"

2 rows will be affected
%fs ls dbfs:/user/hive/warehouse/employess
this will now have 6 files as spark created 2 more files instead changing the already existing files
and it will make the older 2 files invalid
describe detail employess
	numfiles = 4 as spark will consider current version files

describe history employess
it will now show three versions - create table, write and update

%fs ls 'dbfs:/user/hive/warehouse/employess/_delta_log'

this will show three json files representing three diff transactions and three .orc files having checksum of json files

lets read most recent json file
%fs head 'dbfs:/user/hive/warehouse/employess/_delta_log/000000000000002.json'

it will show as 2 files are removed and 2 files are added

advanced features:
=================
Time travel
	audit data chnages
	DESCRIBE HISTORY EMPLOYESS
	query older version
	select *from employess TIMESTAMP AS OF "2019-01-01"
	select *from employess VERSION AS OF 36
	select *from employessv@36
Rollback versions
	RESTORE TABLE employess to TIMESTAMP AS OF "2019-01-01"
	RESTORE TABLE employess to VERSION AS OF 36
	
compact small files
	OPTIMIZE employess
	OPTIMIZE employess ZORDER BY column_name
		this is to index the files
		ex: 5 files and id columns as follows
		file1 : 55,10
		file2: 1,99
		file3 : 15,50
		file4: 75,30
		file5: 8, 100 now the o/p will be 2 files 1 with 1 to 50 and other one with 51 to 100

vacuum a delta table
	clean up unused data files
	files that are no longer in latest table state
	
	VACUUM employess [retention period] / it will delete all the files which are older than retention period specified
	default 7 days
	vacuum = no time travel
	even after we did vacuum, we will have unused files till the retention period mentioned.just to support any long running jobs

DROP table employess / table will be dropped and also data will be gone

databases = schemas in hive metastore
	default db
	/user/hive/warehouse/
create schema/database db_y
location 'dbfs:/custom/path/db_y.db

managed
	hive manages both data and metadata
	by default managed
external
	hive does not maintain data but just the metadata
	if you specify a location when creating the table
	
describe extended table;

delta lake tables:
==================

CTAS statement

create table table_1 AS select *from table_2

create table table_1
comment "contains PII"
partitioned  by (city, birth_date)
location '/some/path'
as select id, name, email, birth_date, city from users

not null constraints
check constraints

cloning:
deep clone
	create table tgt_table deep clone source_table // fully copies data and metadata from a source table to a target
	can sync chnages
	take a quite qa while for large datasets as it will copy full data

shallow clone 
	quickly create a copy of a table
	just copy the delta tx logs
	create table table_one shallow clone src_table

usuful to set up tables for testing in dev
in either case, data modifications will not affect the source

views
=======
just a virtual table

stored views
	create view view_name as query
	persisted in DB
temp views
	create temp view view_name as query
	attached to the spark session/notebook
	dropped when session ends
global temp views
	create global temp view view_name as query
	select *from global_temp.view_name
	attached to the cluster
	dropped when cluster is restarted
	show tables in global_temp;
	
Query files:
============
select *from file_format.`/path/to/file`
	
select *from json.`/path/to/file`
select *from text.`/path/to/file` (CSV, TSV, TXT, JSON)
ex: in csv file, there is a delimiter ; instead , so above one will not work as we cannot pass any options to provide delimiter


create table table_name as select *from json.`/path/to/file`
this does not support file options so we below to do that

create table table_name
(col_name1 col_type1, ...)
using csv
options (header = "true",
		delimiter = ";")
location = path

create table table_name
(col_name1 col_type1, ...)
using JDBC
options(url = "jdbc:squite://hostname:port",
				dbtable = "database.table",
				user="username",
				password="pwd")
limitation: its not a delta table
			we can not expect the performance guarantees associated with delta lake and lakehouse
			having a huge database table

solution to above
================ 

create a temp view temp_view_name and create a table as select *from temp_view_name

spark streaming
================

data stream:
	any data stream that grows over time
	new files landing in cloud storage
	updates to a database captured in a CDC field
	events queued in a pub/sub messaging feed

processing data stream:
	Reprocess the entire source dataset each time
	only process those new data added since last update
		structured streaming

input table / location of source data:
streamDF = spark.readStream.table("input_table")
target table:
streamDF.writeStream \
		.trigger(processingTime="2 minutes") \
		.outputMode("append") \
		.option("checkpointLocation", "/path") \
		.table("target_table")
		
default: processingTime="500ms"
fixed interval : processingTime="2 minutes"
trigger batch: .trigger(once=True) /process all available data in a single batch and stop
trigger micro batches: .trigger(availableNow=True) /process all available data in a multiple micro batches and stop
.outputMode("append") / incrementally append to the target with each batch
.outputMode("complete") /the target table is overwritten with each batch


Guarantees:
1. Fault Tolerance
	checkpointing + write-ahead logs
		record the offset range of data being processed during each trigger interval
2. exactly-once guarantee
	idempotent tasks

unsupported operations:
	sorting and deduping is not supported by streaming dataframe but can be acheived by watermarking and windowing
	
%run ../Includes/Copy-Datasets
(spark.readStream.table("books").createOrReplaceTempView("books_streaming_tmp_vw"))

select *from books_streaming_tmp_vw

Incremental data ingestion:
============================

2 ways
	copy into
		SQL command
		idempotent and incrementally load new data files
		use when we have thousands of files
	auto loader
		structured streaming
		can process billions of files
		support near real-time ingestion of millions of files per hour.
		
		streamDF = spark.readStream \
							.format("cloudfiles") \
							.option("cloudfiles.format", <source_format>) \
							.load('/path/to/files')
						.writeStream \
							.option("checkpointLocation", <checkpoint_directory>)
							.table(<table_name>)
							
Multi-Hop Arch:
================
also called as medalion arch
bronze
	ingest raw data
silver
	filter, cleaned and augmented data
gold
	aggregated data
	
